class Account {
    protected double balance;
    Account(double balance) { this.balance = balance; } // parent constructor

    String describe() { return "Balance: " + balance; }
}

class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private double rate;

    SavingsAccount(double balance, double rate) {
        super(balance);     // super(): MUST be the first statement
        this.rate = rate;
    }

    @Override
    String describe() {
        return super.describe() + ", rate: " + rate; // extend, don't replace
    }
}

public class SuperDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new SavingsAccount(1000, 0.04).describe());
        // prints: Balance: 1000.0, rate: 0.04
    }
}
```
`super(...)` initializes the parent first; `super.describe()` reuses the parent's logic instead of discarding it.

